---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import Crt from '../components/Crt.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import { REPO } from '../lib/site';

const boot = `Boot
: hp(0,0)unix
280120+62356+81364 start 0x11c
Unix Edition 8 -- ipnx Release 1.0 (2026-08-16)
built Sun Aug 16 07:48:53 2026
real mem  = 8388608
avail mem = 7294976
hp0: RP07
il0 at uba0 csr 164000 vec 400
network: il0 10.0.2.15, gateway 10.0.2.2

login:`;
---

<PageLayout
  title="The machine — how ipnx works"
  description="Two emulated computers and a wire between them: a VAX-11/780 running the Research Unix kernel, and a DMD 5620 bitmapped terminal with its own processor."
  eyebrow="Architecture"
  heading="The machine"
  intro="Two computers and a wire between them, which is exactly what sat on a desk at Bell Labs in 1985."
>
  <div class="py-8">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Most retrocomputing apps are one emulator and a screen. ipnx is two emulators, because the
        thing being reproduced was two machines. The Blit — sold by Teletype as the DMD 5620 — was
        not a display. It was a computer with its own processor and memory, connected to a
        headless host by a serial line, and programs ran <em>on it</em> after being downloaded
        <em>into it</em> over that line.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <!-- The diagram -->
    <figure class="my-12">
      <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-rule bg-card p-6">
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 900 260"
          class="mx-auto block h-auto w-full min-w-[680px] max-w-[860px] text-ink"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Block diagram: a VAX-11/780 running Research Unix connects over a DZ11 serial line to a DMD 5620 terminal, which draws to a Metal screen; the VAX also connects to an Interlan Ethernet card and a network file system server on the Mac."
        >
          <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
            <!-- VAX -->
            <rect x="20" y="40" width="250" height="120" />
            <!-- 5620 -->
            <rect x="630" y="40" width="250" height="120" />
            <!-- wire -->
            <line x1="270" y1="100" x2="630" y2="100" />
            <!-- host services -->
            <rect x="20" y="196" width="250" height="46" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />
            <line x1="145" y1="160" x2="145" y2="196" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />
          </g>

          <g class="fill-current font-sans" font-size="13">
            <text x="36" y="62" font-weight="700">VAX-11/780</text>
            <text x="36" y="82" class="opacity-70">open-simh · C interpreter</text>
            <text x="36" y="104" class="opacity-70">Research Unix Edition 8 kernel</text>
            <text x="36" y="126" class="opacity-70">RP07 disk · DZ11 lines · NI1010</text>
            <text x="36" y="148" class="opacity-55" font-size="11">Headless. No screen of its own.</text>

            <text x="646" y="62" font-weight="700">DMD 5620</text>
            <text x="646" y="82" class="opacity-70">dmd_core · WE32100 · Rust</text>
            <text x="646" y="104" class="opacity-70">Firmware 8;7;3 · 1 MB RAM</text>
            <text x="646" y="126" class="opacity-70">800×1024×1 framebuffer</text>
            <text x="646" y="148" class="opacity-55" font-size="11">Runs mux, jim — downloaded.</text>

            <text x="36" y="218" class="opacity-70">Your Mac</text>
            <text x="36" y="234" class="opacity-55" font-size="11">
              netfs server · NAT to the Internet
            </text>
          </g>

          <!-- wire label -->
          <g class="font-mono" font-size="11">
            <rect x="378" y="86" width="146" height="28" class="fill-card" stroke="none" />
            <text x="451" y="104" text-anchor="middle" class="fill-current opacity-70">
              serial line · 9600 ×32
            </text>
          </g>

          <g class="fill-current font-sans opacity-55" font-size="11">
            <text x="290" y="130">DZ11 line 0</text>
            <text x="560" y="130" text-anchor="end">DUART port A</text>
            <text x="155" y="182">netfs / TCP</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 font-sans text-[13px] text-muted">
        One process. Two interpreters, each on its own thread, joined by a loopback socket that
        stands in for the RS-232 cable.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <Prose>
      <h2>Why emulate the whole machine</h2>
      <p>
        The alternative — translating 1985 system calls into modern ones, the way iSH does — runs
        into a wall on Apple platforms: apps may not spawn processes and may not generate code at
        runtime. A Unix that cannot <code>fork</code> is not a Unix.
      </p>
      <p>
        Full-system emulation dissolves the problem rather than fighting it. The Research Unix
        kernel forks its processes <em>inside</em> the emulated VAX, where the host operating
        system has no opinion about it, and both emulator cores are ordinary ahead-of-time
        compiled interpreters — the same pattern App Store review has accepted from UTM SE,
        iDOS and the SIMH-derived iAltair.
      </p>

      <h2>What actually boots</h2>
      <p>
        Not a prepared script. The boot block finds <code>hp(0,0)unix</code>, the kernel probes
        its hardware, <code>/etc/rc</code> mounts the filesystems and brings the network up, and
        <code>init</code> starts a <code>getty</code> on each terminal line:
      </p>

      <Crt text={boot} caption="A cold boot, abridged only in the middle." />

      <h2>The wire</h2>
      <p>
        The two emulators talk over a loopback socket. That is a stand-in for the cable, and it
        turned out to be the least interesting part of the problem: the throttles were at each
        end, not in the middle. The 5620's DUART paced characters at its programmed baud rate,
        and the simulator's serial device rate-limited the socket to whatever speed the guest
        kernel asked for. Both were 9600 baud, in series, which is exactly as slow as it sounds.
      </p>
      <p>
        Raising both took the <code>mux</code> download from about a hundred seconds to fifteen.
        The lesson generalises: when something is slow at both ends, fixing one end buys you
        almost nothing, and the measurement that tells you so is queue depth rather than
        throughput.
      </p>

      <h2>Idling</h2>
      <p>
        A simulator that is doing nothing should cost nothing, and this one did not — a stock
        <code>vax780</code> burns most of a core sitting at a login prompt. The fix was three
        flags upstream had not set, on the periodic units that sit at the head of the event
        queue. Patched, the VAX thread idles at under three per cent with the guest's clock still
        keeping correct time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The 5620 is the honest remaining cost. It does not idle, because a real one did not: its
        processor genuinely spun. Closing its window stops the thread and returns the core.
      </p>

      <h2>Read the rest</h2>
      <p>
        The full technical record is in the <a href={REPO}>repository</a> — the architecture spec,
        the phase notes, the network file system wire format, and a long list of the mistakes that
        produced each of the rules above. Nothing in it is summarised for effect; it was written
        while the thing was being built.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <p class="mt-10">
      <a
        href={href('/manual')}
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border-2 border-ink px-5 py-2.5 font-sans text-[14px] font-bold text-ink transition hover:bg-ink hover:text-paper"
      >
        Read the manual
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
